@KRASTM 
Thanks for the reply. Just to be clear: we run the code from XSPI2 @100MHz in Memory Mapped Mode. But communicating to another peripheral on SPI5.
I will try reducing the SPI5 clock, and check if that will work

Edit: After setting the APB clock to 18 MHz (and the output clock to the same) I still get mismatches.
I think the function HAL_SPI_TransmitReceive need to wait for an RXP flag after sending. The code _might_ be too fast and the received data is not yet available at the check

I've run a separate test with a simple counter in the function HAL_SPI_TransmitReceive between sending and receiving.
```
      static uint32_t spi_tx_cnt = 0;
      while( (__HAL_SPI_GET_FLAG(hspi, SPI_FLAG_RXP)) == 0 ) ++spi_tx_cnt;

      /* Check the RXP flag */
      if ((__HAL_SPI_GET_FLAG(hspi, SPI_FLAG_RXP)) && (initial_RxXferCount > 0UL))
```

This spi_tx_cnt increases in value, so without this loop the if-statement will be skipped, 
This means the initial_RxXferCount is not lowered, and a new byte is sent. But no new byte is received and the initial_RxXferCount will never reach 0 resulting in a timeout

Can you verify this on your part?

body:
When receiving in SPI Master, full duplex mode using 
HAL_StatusTypeDef HAL_SPI_Receive(SPI_HandleTypeDef *hspi, uint8_t *pData, uint16_t Size, uint32_t Timeout)

```
  if ((hspi->Init.Mode == SPI_MODE_MASTER) && (hspi->Init.Direction == SPI_DIRECTION_2LINES))
  {
    hspi->State = HAL_SPI_STATE_BUSY_RX;
    /* Call transmit-receive function to send Dummy data on Tx line and generate clock on CLK line */
    return HAL_SPI_TransmitReceive(hspi, pData, pData, Size, Timeout);
  }
```

In the H7rsxx library, this check is removed. When manually adding this check the code will run as intended
We mainly notice these errors at larger messages (7+ bytes), or smaller prescaler (16-)
